  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25d-perchlike-fishes-4-south-american-dwarf-cichlids/teleocichla_centrarchus_en-2/",
      "html": "<p>Teleocichla centrarchus from the Rio Xingu is probably the most imported Teleocichla. In addition, it is the type species of this genus. As the males reach a maximum length of approx. 10 cm, they can certainly still be called dwarf cichlids. Care and even breeding of T. centrarchus is not too difficult, as long as you pay attention to correct nutrition.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25d-perchlike-fishes-4-south-american-dwarf-cichlids/teleocichla_centrarchus_en-2/",
      "html": "<p>Brazil, Rio Xingu.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25d-perchlike-fishes-4-south-american-dwarf-cichlids/teleocichla_centrarchus_en-2/",
      "html": "<p>They greedily eat large chunks like Krill, Mysis or red bloodworms, but they stuff so much into themselves that they will die from it sooner or later. It is better to feed them small live or frozen food like Artemia (frozen or newly hatched), Daphnia, Cyclops or lobster eggs.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25d-perchlike-fishes-4-south-american-dwarf-cichlids/teleocichla_centrarchus_en-2/",
      "html": "<p>Since they are peaceful towards other species and do not destroy plants either, they can thoroughly be kept in a community tank, provided that the right nutrition can be guaranteed.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25d-perchlike-fishes-4-south-american-dwarf-cichlids/teleocichla_centrarchus_en-2/",
      "html": "<p>The females, mostly only 8 cm maximum, are a little smaller than the males and soon show a pink belly, by which they signal their readiness to spawn to the male.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25d-perchlike-fishes-4-south-american-dwarf-cichlids/teleocichla_centrarchus_en-2/",
      "html": "<p>Breeding is not difficult, as long as you offer these fish a flat and tight cave to deposit their eggs and keep them in soft or medium-hard water.</p>"
    }
  ],
